WAL-logged major-version upgrades
================================

pg_upgrade prepares the new cluster while both versions are available. It
records storage operations and file captures as text commands in
pg_upgrade_relink.tmp. After the prepared data is synced, the frontend
publishes that file as pg_upgrade_relink and leaves the new cluster stopped.
Initialization and catalog-restore WAL are discarded before this publication.

The startup process consumes the commands on the first normal primary start,
before accepting connections or launching background database writers. It
emits START, RELINK and RAWFILE records together with ordinary relation WAL,
then emits COMPLETE and takes the completion shutdown checkpoint. Upgrade
records have transaction ID zero; there is no upgrade transaction or COMMIT.

RELINK describes explicit INHERIT and RECREATE operations. Logged relations
can inherit their old files with link, clone or swap transfer. Copy and
copy-file-range transfers rebuild them with ordinary SMGR create records and
full-page images. New, rebuilt and unlogged relations are recreated in every
mode. Logged relations also capture their free-space and visibility-map forks,
including when their main forks are inherited, so later WAL can reference those
pages. Per-database batches end before another record type is emitted. File
existence, command input, and physical-layout checks remain necessary.

Before startup can emit WAL or change migrated slots, it durably sets the
reserved backupEndPoint value PG_INT64_MAX. The existing control-file layout
is unchanged. This marker rejects an interrupted emission even if the input
file is subsequently lost. Startup clears it only after the completion
checkpoint and durable removal of pg_upgrade_relink. A partially emitted
target cannot be resumed; a retry needs a fresh target and intact old storage.
The usual source-reuse restrictions for link and swap transfers still apply.

The initial redo position retains all startup and upgrade WAL until completion.
Migrated logical slots receive a new decoding snapshot and positions within
that WAL before their state is saved. Emission rejects unexpected files or
input commands rather than treating an incomplete target as a normal cluster.


Standby and archive replay
--------------------------

pg_upgrade.signal selects upgrade recovery. A streaming skeleton also uses
standby.signal and pg_upgrade_standby_old_datadir; archive recovery uses
recovery.signal after old-version recovery reaches the final old checkpoint.
START binds the retained files to their source identity, timeline, checkpoint,
and physical layout. Archive startup selects a compatible staged upgrade
window and keeps the old archive control metadata needed for those checks.

RELINK restores inherited files or recreates target storage, RAWFILE restores
auxiliary files, and ordinary WAL restores relation pages. Replay adopts the
new primary's checksum settings through the server's checksum-state path,
including the shared cache and local state used by page readers and writers.
COMPLETE closes the window directly, without a transaction COMMIT. Recovery
cannot serve hot-standby queries, promote or finish until the following shutdown
checkpoint is durable as a restartpoint. The persistent control marker rejects
restarting a partially applied target, including after COMPLETE without that
restartpoint. A finalized target can restart using its saved recovery progress.

Bootstrap streaming does not consume a replication slot. Operators must keep
upgrade WAL and the retained old data available for the whole replay. Transfer
mode requirements apply to inherited files; copy keeps its source, while link
and swap have their usual source-reuse restrictions.


Automatic standby handoff
-------------------------

Before the final old-primary shutdown, pg_upgrade checks that every required
persistent physical slot has a valid restart position and a connected streaming
standby. It starts a guarded smart shutdown, durably arms the request, rechecks
the slot set, and then releases the guard. HANDOFF needs matching support in the
old primary and its standbys. Their configured replication listeners remain
available during this phase; ordinary binary-upgrade starts keep TCP disabled.

The old primary durably removes pg_upgrade_handoff.pending before emitting
HANDOFF immediately before its shutdown checkpoint. It waits for each required
slot to confirm durable receipt of the checkpoint and saves those positions.
A standby persists the checkpoint as a restartpoint, then pauses. Restarting the
old-version standby restores the pause. pg_wal_replay_resume() durably cancels
that HANDOFF before acknowledging the request; cancellation preserves a later
manual pause and does not suppress a future HANDOFF. Promotion is blocked while
HANDOFF is active.

Failed frontend shutdown leaves a fallback stop available. A target that still
runs after both stop attempts keeps its data directory. An interrupted HANDOFF
must be resolved against the old primary and standby state before retrying;
do not remove a request file while a postmaster could still consume it.


Cascaded standbys and WAL retention
----------------------------------

Primary completion confirms receipt by its direct standbys. Each relay also
waits for its own persistent physical slots before declaring its HANDOFF pause
complete. An offline leaf therefore keeps the relay waiting, including across
relay restart, while the primary can finish after receiving the relay's receipt.

pg_upgrade recreates the primary's physical slots. First startup reserves their
WAL at the bootstrap redo position before emitting upgrade records. A new relay
validates its retained old slot files and reserves slots for its children before
replay can checkpoint or recycle WAL. Slot names and reservations are local to
each parent; they are not transmitted in upgrade WAL.

Keep fsync enabled, max_slot_wal_keep_size = -1, and
idle_replication_slot_timeout = 0 while these reservations are inactive. Each
parent needs enough max_replication_slots and wal_level = replica or higher.
These settings are checked before primary HANDOFF and again during migration.
Bootstrap every new standby without consuming the migrated upstream slot and
keep wal_receiver_create_temp_slot disabled. Once all nodes have replayed the
upgrade, activate primary_slot_name from the leaves upward. This order preserves
the replay-start WAL for descendants that have not yet finished.
